# met-timeseries

Meteorological timeseries downloading and processing for hydrologic models.

## Overview

`met-timeseries` downloads gridded climate data (NLDAS-2, PRISM) and
aggregates it to user-defined **metzone** polygons derived from catchment
vector files, producing per-metzone monthly timeseries stored as Parquet files.

### Key concepts

| Term | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Catchment** | An individual polygon in the input vector file (e.g. a HUC-12 sub-basin). |
| **Metzone** | A group of catchments sharing the same `metzone_id`.  All catchments in a metzone are dissolved into a single polygon before data are fetched. |
| **Timeseries** | Monthly aggregated values for one metzone and one variable, stored as a Parquet file. |

## Installation

```bash
pip install met-timeseries
```

Or from source:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Mgetta/met-timeseries.git
cd met-timeseries
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

## Quick start

### Python API

```python
from met_timeseries.config import PipelineConfig
from met_timeseries.pipeline import run_pipeline

config = PipelineConfig(
    catchment_path="catchments.gpkg",   # shapefile, GeoPackage, GeoJSON, …
    metzone_column="metzone_id",         # column that groups catchments
    output_dir="output",
    start_year=2000,
    end_year=2005,
)
run_pipeline(config)
```

### CLI

```bash
met-timeseries \
    --catchment-path catchments.gpkg \
    --metzone-column metzone_id \
    --output-dir output \
    --start-year 2000 \
    --end-year 2005
```

Run `met-timeseries --help` for a full list of options.

## Input vector format

The catchment file can be any format that GeoPandas supports:
`.shp`, `.gpkg`, `.geojson`, `.parquet`, or any GDAL/OGR driver.

The file must contain a column (default: `metzone_id`) whose values identify
which metzone each catchment belongs to.  Catchments sharing the same value
are dissolved into a single analysis polygon.

Example: an input file with 1,020 catchments across 68 metzones produces
**68 dissolved polygons**, one timeseries per metzone per variable.

## Output structure

```
output/
└── nldas/
    └── <metzone_id>/
        └── <variable>/
            └── <year>_<month>.parquet
```

Each Parquet file contains a `metzone_id` column plus the aggregated variable
values for that month.

## Procedural API

The pipeline is intentionally procedural — no abstract base classes or heavy
inheritance.  You can call individual steps directly:

```python
from met_timeseries.polygons import load_polygons
from met_timeseries.ledger import load_ledger, mark_complete, is_complete, get_incomplete
from met_timeseries.pipeline import process_nldas_month, process_prism_month, assemble_final_output
from met_timeseries.io import save_timeseries, load_timeseries
from met_timeseries.aggregation import aggregate_over_polygon
```

## Data sources

| Source | Description | Access |
|--------|-------------|--------|
| **NLDAS-2** | Hourly forcing data (precipitation, temperature, radiation, …) at 1/8° resolution | NASA GES DISC — requires Earthdata login |
| **PRISM** | Monthly climate normals (precipitation, Tmax, Tmin) at 4 km | Oregon State PRISM Climate Group |

## Progress ledger

The pipeline tracks completed (step, year, month) combinations in a CSV
ledger file so interrupted runs can resume without re-downloading data.

```python
from met_timeseries.ledger import is_complete, get_incomplete

remaining = get_incomplete("ledger.csv", "nldas", 2000, 2005)
print(f"{len(remaining)} months remaining")
```

